# Cross-Origin

## Introduction

Sooner or later you may need to crop an image that lives on another origin: a different domain, port or protocol than the page running the cropper. A CDN, a cloud bucket or a user's avatar on a third-party service are typical cases.

Browsers treat such images with suspicion. A page may _display_ an image from anywhere, but scripts on the page may not _read its pixels_ unless the image's server explicitly allows it. The rules are called [Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS).

The reason is privacy. Suppose you are signed in to site A, which serves your private scans. Site B can embed one of those scans in an `<img>`, and your browser will happily send your site A cookies along. If site B's scripts could then read the pixels, they could copy your documents. CORS stops that, and it applies to every cross-origin image, private or not, unless its server opts in.

## How does it relate to cropper?

There are two ways to produce a cropped image, and CORS affects only one of them.

### Client-side

The usual approach is to crop in the browser: draw the selected area on a canvas with `getCanvas()`, turn it into a blob or data URL, and upload the result. Drawing a cross-origin image that the server hasn't approved "taints" the canvas, and from then on `toBlob()` and `toDataURL()` throw a `SecurityError`. No cropper setting can get around this. The only fix is on the server that hosts the image (see [Configuring the storage](#configuring-the-storage)).

The table shows what happens with the default `crossOrigin` setting (`true`) and with `crossOrigin={false}`:

| Image source | Server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` | `crossOrigin` | Image shows | `getCanvas()` result is usable |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Same origin | not needed | any | yes | yes |
| Other origin | yes | `true` (default) | yes | yes |
| Other origin | no | `true` (default) | **no**, `onError` fires | no |
| Other origin | no | `false` | yes | **no**, the canvas is tainted |

### Server-side

The other approach is to let the user choose the area in the browser, then send the image URL together with the coordinates (`cropper.getCoordinates()`) to your backend and crop there. Servers aren't bound by CORS, so this works with any image.

For this approach, turn off the canvas and the CORS request:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Cropper, type CropperRef } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let { src }: { src: string } = $props();
	let cropper: CropperRef | undefined = $state();

	async function save() {
		const coordinates = cropper?.getCoordinates();
		await fetch('/api/crop', {
			method: 'POST',
			body: JSON.stringify({ src, coordinates })
		});
	}
</script>

<Cropper bind:this={cropper} {src} canvas={false} crossOrigin={false} />
```

<Admonition type="note" title="Why both props?">

`canvas={false}` stops the cropper from rendering its internal canvas, so `getCanvas()` returns `null`. It doesn't change how the image is requested: `crossOrigin` defaults to `true` regardless, and a server without CORS headers would then refuse the image. `crossOrigin={false}` loads it as a plain image instead.

</Admonition>

## The `crossOrigin` prop

`crossOrigin` controls the `crossorigin` attribute of the image the cropper loads and displays:

| Value | Request |
| --- | --- |
| `true` (default) or `'anonymous'` | CORS request without credentials (no cookies). |
| `'use-credentials'` | CORS request with cookies. The server must reply with your exact origin (not `*`) and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. |
| `false` | Plain image request, no CORS. Fine for display, but the canvas will be tainted for cross-origin images. |

The same prop exists on [`CropperBackgroundImage`](/docs/components/CropperBackgroundImage), [`CropperSource`](/docs/components/CropperSource) and the [`useCropperImage`](/docs/hooks/useCropperImage) hook. If you write a custom background component, forward it to your `<img>` the same way.

## Configuring the storage

For client-side cropping, the server that hosts the images has to allow your site to read them. It does this by adding the [`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Access-Control-Allow-Origin) header to image responses:

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://website-with-cropper.me
```

<Admonition type="tip" title="Cached responses">

If the same image URL is also shown elsewhere on your site without `crossorigin`, the browser may reuse that cached, non-CORS response and the cropper's request will fail. Have the server send `Vary: Origin` as well, or load the image through a distinct URL.

</Admonition>

If you can't change the storage settings, crop on the server as described above, or serve the images through a proxy on your own origin.

How to set up CORS on popular storage services:

- [Google Cloud Storage](https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/configuring-cors)
- [Amazon S3](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cors.html)
